## Step 1: Generate Clip1 + Clip2 in Google Flow

Captions are **not** overlaid afterward with ffmpeg — Makari wants them baked directly into the Google Flow generation. When writing the Flow prompt for each clip, include the exact on-screen caption line as part of the scene description (bold white sans-serif, black outline, centered in the lower third — this is what Flow already renders when asked for on-screen text). Pick one short, punchy lyric or theme line per clip that reflects that clip's beat in the song (e.g. Clip1 = struggle/before, Clip2 = triumph/after — see `Clip1_Struggle.mp4` / `Clip2_Triumph.mp4` naming).

Specs to match:
- 720x1280 (9:16 vertical), h264, 24fps
- Clip length varies by song — size Clip1 and Clip2 so that Clip1 + Clip2 + Outro roughly equals the length of the chorus-trimmed audio clip you'll use in Step 5. Don't force every video to exactly 30 seconds; let the chorus length drive it.

Name the files descriptively, e.g. `Clip1_<Theme>.mp4`, `Clip2_<Theme>.mp4`, matching the pattern already used for Crown in the Furnace.

## Step 2: Reuse the Outro asset

`Outro.mp4` in the ATG Malachi Visuals folder is a fixed, reusable brand asset (currently 720x1280, 24fps, ~10s) — don't regenerate it per song. Just confirm it's still present before assembling.

## Step 3: Get chorus-trimmed audio

The song's raw Suno export (e.g. `<Song Name>.mp3`) is the full track. For the video you need a **chorus-trimmed** cut — a shorter clip that starts at the chorus/hook, saved alongside it as `<Song Name> (Starts at Chorus).mp3`. Trim this before the final merge step; it should be roughly the same length as Clip1 + Clip2 + Outro combined.

## Step 4: Stitch Clip1 + Clip2 + Outro

Since all three segments share codec/resolution/framerate, use the concat demuxer (fast, no re-encode):

```bash
cat > filelist.txt <<EOF
file 'Clip1_<Theme>.mp4'
file 'Clip2_<Theme>.mp4'
file 'Outro.mp4'
EOF
ffmpeg -y -f concat -safe 0 -i filelist.txt -c copy "<Song Name>_FINAL.mp4"
```

If ffmpeg complains about mismatched codec parameters between segments, fall back to re-encoding instead of `-c copy`:
```bash
ffmpeg -y -f concat -safe 0 -i filelist.txt -c:v libx264 -pix_fmt yuv420p -r 24 "<Song Name>_FINAL.mp4"
```

## Step 5: Merge in the chorus-trimmed audio (final step)

This is always the last edit made to the video — confirmed working pattern:

```bash
ffmpeg -y -i "<Song Name>_FINAL.mp4" -i "<Song Name> (Starts at Chorus).mp3" \
  -map 0:v -map 1:a -c:v copy -c:a aac -b:a 192k -shortest \
  "<Song Name>_FINAL_WithAudio.mp4"
```

`-shortest` trims to whichever of video/audio is shorter, so it's forgiving if Step 3's trim isn't frame-perfect. This `_FINAL_WithAudio.mp4` file is the actual deliverable — save it in the ATG Malachi Visuals folder.

## Step 6: Compress for upload

Anything uploaded through the Chrome extension's `file_upload` tool is capped at 10MB combined, and the raw merged video usually comes in well over that. Re-encode a smaller copy specifically for upload — don't touch the original:

```bash
ffmpeg -y -i "<Song Name>_FINAL_WithAudio.mp4" \
  -c:v libx264 -b:v 2000k -maxrate 2200k -bufsize 4000k -preset medium -pix_fmt yuv420p \
  -c:a aac -b:a 128k \
  "<Song Name>_FINAL_ForUpload.mp4"
```

This got Crown in the Furnace from ~15MB down to ~8MB with no visible quality loss. If the source is unusually long/large, lower `-b:v` further rather than skipping compression.

## Step 7: Publish to YouTube via Blotato

Blotato's own upload API rejects direct file PUTs from this sandbox (network-blocked), and Blotato's `create_post` API has no fields for tags/category/custom thumbnail. So: drive Blotato's **web UI** through the browser, not the raw API.

1. Copy the `_FINAL_ForUpload.mp4` file into the outputs scratchpad path before calling `file_upload` — the mounted ATG Malachi Visuals folder is not on the Chrome extension's accepted-source allowlist, but the scratchpad is. Uploading straight from the project folder will silently fail with "Claude in Chrome is not connected."
2. If a description field gets corrupted mid-type (text lands in the wrong spot), click into the field, Cmd+A, Delete, and retype clean rather than trying to patch it.
3. Title/description: write a hook-driven, SEO-optimized title and description with a controversial or provocative angle — the kind of framing that makes someone stop scrolling and argue in the comments (a bold claim, a callout, an "unpopular opinion" framing, etc.), while staying true to the song's actual theme. Don't force keywords or controversy that don't fit the song. **Do not link to Suno.**
4. **Always link the related long-form video** in the description — every short/vertical Malachi video corresponds to a long-form YouTube upload, and the description must point there (plain URL or "Full song ⬇" style callout). If that long-form video hasn't been published yet, publish or locate it first rather than skipping the link — don't treat it as optional the way a Spotify/Apple link is.
5. Settings to confirm before publishing (ask Makari if any of these differ from the defaults below):
   - Privacy: Public
   - Notify subscribers: Yes
   - Playlist: add to the "Chosen In The Fire 🔥" playlist
   - Channel/account: "Malachi (Atg Malachi)"

## Known gotchas

- **Suno tab hangs**: a long-lived Suno.com tab will eventually start throwing "Script injection timed out" / stuck document_idle errors after several interactions. Don't fight it — open a fresh tab and re-navigate; this reliably clears it.
- **Suno download menu**: on the song page, "..." → "Download" → "MP3 Audio" gets the audio (WAV/Stems/Video are Pro-gated). The small circular icon next to "Edit" downloads the cover image, not audio — don't confuse the two.
- **file_upload source folder**: only accepts files from certain shared locations (the outputs scratchpad works; the mounted project folder does not), regardless of file size. Always stage the upload file in the scratchpad first.
- **Direct bash curl/API calls** to Blotato or Google's OAuth endpoints are blocked by the sandbox network proxy — anything hitting those domains has to go through the real browser instead.

## Where things live

All source and finished assets live in the ATG Malachi Visuals folder: raw song MP3s, `Lyrics - <Song>.txt` files, `Clip1_*`/`Clip2_*`/`Outro.mp4`, and the `_FINAL`/`_FINAL_WithAudio`/`_FINAL_ForUpload` outputs per song. If direct YouTube Data API access is ever needed (tags, category, custom thumbnail, editing an already-published video), the OAuth credentials are saved in `YouTube API Credentials (KEEP PRIVATE).txt` in that same folder — mint access tokens through the browser (OAuth Playground), not bash, since `oauth2.googleapis.com` is also proxy-blocked.
